WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.957
- [Announcer] Have you ever
looked around and wondered,

00:00:02.957 --> 00:00:06.010
"Where does all of my stuff come from?"

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:11.010
Maybe you have a pencil that
looks something like this.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.790
Now, lots of different things
go into making a pencil,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:18.190
but we can break it down
into a few basic parts.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:19.110
For example,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.420
the wood that makes up
the body of the pencil

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.810
comes from trees.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.780
Most pencil erasers nowadays are made from

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:29.690
some kind of plastic,
which is refined from oil.

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:32.410
And this aluminum ring here
at the base of the pencil

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.390
and the graphite tip at
the top are both made

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.263
from different minerals.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:44.650
Everything we use can be traced
back to natural resources,

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:48.823
which are things provided by
the Earth and its processes.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.300
Some natural resources that we use a lot

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.990
in our day-to-day lives
are trees, fresh water,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:01.123
minerals, and fossil fuels like oil.

00:01:02.358 --> 00:01:05.170
We can divide these different
types of natural resources

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.270
into renewable resources

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.640
and nonrenewable resources.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.750
Renewable resources can be replenished

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.330
in a relatively short amount of time.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.760
Now, I say relatively
short because in some cases

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.840
it still might take quite a
few years for them to renew.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.360
For example, if you have a
tree and you cut it down,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.240
but you plant a new
tree to take its place,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.470
you're still gonna have
to wait let's say 20 years

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.880
before you can have
another fully grown tree.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.500
Nonrenewable resources
are often very limited

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.670
and can take much longer
than a human lifetime

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.040
for the earth to replenish them.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:44.873
For example,

00:01:44.873 --> 00:01:46.970
when carbon is compressed
for millions of years,

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.370
it can turn into a diamond.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.270
Once we mine that
diamond out of the earth,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.610
it'll take millions of
years for another diamond

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:56.290
to be formed that's the same size.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.350
Now, just because a resource is renewable,

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.920
doesn't mean we can use as much
as we want whenever we want.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.810
We know that trees are a
renewable resource, right?

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:07.140
However, cutting down too many trees,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.360
a process called deforestation,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.860
can have downsides.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.210
Many animals rely on
trees for habitat or food.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.130
And they can't exactly wait 20 years

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.970
for their homes to grow back.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.630
Plus, taking wood out of
the ecosystem and burning it

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:23.070
releases extra carbon
dioxide that's gonna end up

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.010
in the atmosphere,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.680
rather than naturally moving
through the carbon cycle.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.040
This is one of many things

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.993
that contributes to climate change.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.130
We also can't find all the
natural resources we need

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.280
in all of the places where we need them.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.180
Different places have unique geographic

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.490
or geologic features,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.240
like mountains and forests

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.140
or rivers and oceans.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.430
So they all have different resources.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.020
Since natural resources
aren't evenly distributed,

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.930
different regions trade, sell,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:52.470
or exchange their
resources with each other.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.303
For example,

00:02:53.303 --> 00:02:54.900
let's say a community lives at the base

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.150
of these mountains here,

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.830
so they can chop down wood from the forest

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.550
whenever they need it.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:00.910
If they go looking in the mountains,

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.500
they might even find some rare minerals.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.960
Another community down the
road lives near the river,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.080
so they can go fishing every day.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.740
The soil near the river
is also really good

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:10.573
for growing crops.

00:03:10.573 --> 00:03:13.180
So they farm a lot of tasty vegetables.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:14.950
Now let's say the people
in the mountain community

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.530
want to eat the fish, pumpkins,

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.600
and other crops harvested
in the river community.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:20.570
At the same time,

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:22.890
the river community wants
those hard to find minerals

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.240
and wood to make new farm tools.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.390
They'll need to find a way to
share their unique resources

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.990
so everyone gets what they need.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.090
Now try and imagine this
system on a global scale.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.930
The things we rely on every day,

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.230
from the pencils that you use to write,

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.800
to the technologies that
power our communities,

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.970
they all connect us in some
way back to the resources

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.470
that our planet naturally provides.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.020
Take a look around again.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.760
Where in the world did
the things in your life

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.020
really come from?

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.440
Once you break an object
down to its original pieces,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.513
you might be surprised by what you find.

